        "key_prerequisites": [],
        "has_content_in_snapshot": true,
        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "example_question",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Solve the inequality <span class=\"math\">[MATH: -1 &lt; -2x + 1 \\leq 7.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "Solve the inequality [MATH: -1 < -2x + 1 \\leq 7.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>EXPLANATION</p>",
            "readable_text": "EXPLANATION",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "example_explanation",
            "normalized_html": "<p>We isolate <span class=\"math\">[MATH: x]</span> by applying the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \u2212 1|&lt; \u2212 2x + 1 \u2264 7; \u2212 1 \u2212 1|&lt; \u2212 2x + 1 \u2212 1 \u2264 7 \u2212 1; \u2212 2|&lt; \u2212 2x \u2264 6]</span></p><p>At this point, we need to divide by <span class=\"math\">[MATH: -2]</span>. However, because we are dividing by a negative, we also need to flip the inequality signs.</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \u2212 2|&lt; \u2212 2x \u2264 6; (\u2212 2)/(\u2212 2)|&gt;(\u2212 2x)/(\u2212 2)\u2265(6)/(\u2212 2); 1|&gt; x \u2265 \u2212 3]</span></p><p>The solution can be written as <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 1 &gt; x \\geq - 3,]</span> but this is somewhat difficult to read because the larger number is on the left rather than the right. So, we will rewrite the solution by reversing the order of the numbers:</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: -3 \\leq x &lt; 1]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "We isolate [MATH: x] by applying the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n[MATH: \u2212 1|< \u2212 2x + 1 \u2264 7; \u2212 1 \u2212 1|< \u2212 2x + 1 \u2212 1 \u2264 7 \u2212 1; \u2212 2|< \u2212 2x \u2264 6]\n\nAt this point, we need to divide by [MATH: -2]. However, because we are dividing by a negative, we also need to flip the inequality signs.\n\n[MATH: \u2212 2|< \u2212 2x \u2264 6; (\u2212 2)/(\u2212 2)|>(\u2212 2x)/(\u2212 2)\u2265(6)/(\u2212 2); 1|> x \u2265 \u2212 3]\n\nThe solution can be written as [MATH: 1 > x \\geq - 3,] but this is somewhat difficult to read because the larger number is on the left rather than the right. So, we will rewrite the solution by reversing the order of the numbers:\n\n[MATH: -3 \\leq x < 1]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Example Prompt:\nSolve the inequality [MATH: -1 < -2x + 1 \\leq 7.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe isolate [MATH: x] by applying the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n[MATH: \u2212 1|< \u2212 2x + 1 \u2264 7; \u2212 1 \u2212 1|< \u2212 2x + 1 \u2212 1 \u2264 7 \u2212 1; \u2212 2|< \u2212 2x \u2264 6]\n\nAt this point, we need to divide by [MATH: -2]. However, because we are dividing by a negative, we also need to flip the inequality signs.\n\n[MATH: \u2212 2|< \u2212 2x \u2264 6; (\u2212 2)/(\u2212 2)|>(\u2212 2x)/(\u2212 2)\u2265(6)/(\u2212 2); 1|> x \u2265 \u2212 3]\n\nThe solution can be written as [MATH: 1 > x \\geq - 3,] but this is somewhat difficult to read because the larger number is on the left rather than the right. So, we will rewrite the solution by reversing the order of the numbers:\n\n[MATH: -3 \\leq x < 1]"
