      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "step_index": 4,
        "item_type": "step",
        "step_type": "example",
        "step_id": "108828",
        "content_id": "13935",
        "dom_id": "step-108828",
        "selector": "#step-108828",
        "step_anchor": "step-8",
        "title": "Representing Solutions of Compound Inequalities on Number Lines",
        "key_prerequisites": [],
        "has_content_in_snapshot": true,
        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "example_question",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Represent the solution to the inequality <span class=\"math\">[MATH: -3 &lt; 1-2x &lt; 11]</span> using a number line.</p>",
            "readable_text": "Represent the solution to the inequality [MATH: -3 < 1-2x < 11] using a number line.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>EXPLANATION</p>",
            "readable_text": "EXPLANATION",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "example_explanation",
            "normalized_html": "<p>We isolate <span class=\"math\">[MATH: x]</span> by applying the addition and multiplication principles, as usual. </p><p>First, we apply the addition principle:</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \u2212 3|&lt; 1 \u2212 2x &lt; 11; \u2212 3 \u2212 1|&lt; 1 \u2212 2x \u2212 1 &lt; 11 \u2212 1; \u2212 4|&lt; \u2212 2x &lt; 10]</span></p><p>Then, we divide by <span class=\"math\">[MATH: -2.]</span> Note that since we are dividing by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality signs. </p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \u2212 4|&lt; \u2212 2x &lt; 10; (\u2212 4)/(\u2212 2)|&gt; (\u2212 2x)/(\u2212 2) &gt; (10)/(\u2212 2); 2|&gt; x &gt; \u2212 5]</span></p><p>The solution can be written as <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2 &gt; x &gt; -5]</span>, but this is somewhat difficult to read because the larger number is on the left rather than the right. So, we will rewrite the solution by reversing the order of the numbers: </p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: -5 &lt; x &lt; 2]</span></p><p>We can represent this solution using a number line, as follows.</p><div class=\"graphic\"><img src=\"/graphics/692aff95e86f691ddb4f9957282f51c5\"></div>",
            "readable_text": "We isolate [MATH: x] by applying the addition and multiplication principles, as usual.\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle:\n\n[MATH: \u2212 3|< 1 \u2212 2x < 11; \u2212 3 \u2212 1|< 1 \u2212 2x \u2212 1 < 11 \u2212 1; \u2212 4|< \u2212 2x < 10]\n\nThen, we divide by [MATH: -2.] Note that since we are dividing by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality signs.\n\n[MATH: \u2212 4|< \u2212 2x < 10; (\u2212 4)/(\u2212 2)|> (\u2212 2x)/(\u2212 2) > (10)/(\u2212 2); 2|> x > \u2212 5]\n\nThe solution can be written as [MATH: 2 > x > -5], but this is somewhat difficult to read because the larger number is on the left rather than the right. So, we will rewrite the solution by reversing the order of the numbers:\n\n[MATH: -5 < x < 2]\n\nWe can represent this solution using a number line, as follows.\n\n[IMG: /graphics/692aff95e86f691ddb4f9957282f51c5]",
            "images": [
              {
                "src": "/graphics/692aff95e86f691ddb4f9957282f51c5",
                "alt": ""
              }
            ],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [
          {
            "src": "/graphics/692aff95e86f691ddb4f9957282f51c5",
            "alt": ""
          }
        ],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Example Prompt:\nRepresent the solution to the inequality [MATH: -3 < 1-2x < 11] using a number line.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe isolate [MATH: x] by applying the addition and multiplication principles, as usual.\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle:\n\n[MATH: \u2212 3|< 1 \u2212 2x < 11; \u2212 3 \u2212 1|< 1 \u2212 2x \u2212 1 < 11 \u2212 1; \u2212 4|< \u2212 2x < 10]\n\nThen, we divide by [MATH: -2.] Note that since we are dividing by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality signs.\n\n[MATH: \u2212 4|< \u2212 2x < 10; (\u2212 4)/(\u2212 2)|> (\u2212 2x)/(\u2212 2) > (10)/(\u2212 2); 2|> x > \u2212 5]\n\nThe solution can be written as [MATH: 2 > x > -5], but this is somewhat difficult to read because the larger number is on the left rather than the right. So, we will rewrite the solution by reversing the order of the numbers:\n\n[MATH: -5 < x < 2]\n\nWe can represent this solution using a number line, as follows.\n\n[IMG: /graphics/692aff95e86f691ddb4f9957282f51c5]"
      },
