    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nSuppose that we wish to solve the compound inequality\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 4 \\le x + 2 \\le 8.]\n\nContent:\nTo solve the compound inequality, we apply the usual operations to the middle of the inequality to isolate [MATH: x.] Keep in mind that whatever we do to the middle, we also need to do to both the left-hand and right-hand sides.\n\nContent:\nHere, we can isolate [MATH: x] by subtracting [MATH: 2\\mathbin{:}]\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\eqalign{\n4 & \\le x + 2 \\le 8 \\[5pt]\n4 \\,{\\color{red}-}\\, {\\color{red}{2}} &\\le x + 2 \\,{\\color{red}-}\\, {\\color{red}{2}} \\le 8 \\,{\\color{red}-}\\, {\\color{red}{2}} \\[5pt]\n2 &\\le x \\le 6\n}]\n\nContent:\nTherefore, the solution is [MATH: 2 \\le x \\le 6.]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Solving Compound Inequalities\n\nExample Prompt:\nSolve the inequality [MATH: 0 \\leq 3x+6 < 15.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe isolate [MATH: x] by applying the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n[MATH: \\eqalign{\n0 &\\leq 3x+6 < 15 \\[5pt]\n0 - 6 &\\leq 3x + 6 -6 < 15-6 \\[5pt]\n-6 &\\leq 3x < 9 \\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{-6}{3} &\\leq \\dfrac{3x}{3} < \\dfrac{9}{3} \\[5pt]\n-2 &\\leq x < 3\n}]\n\nTherefore, the solution is [MATH: -2 \\leq x < 3.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nDetermine the solution for [MATH: \u2212 2 < x + 3 \u2264 10.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 5 < x \u2264 7]\nb. [MATH: 1 \u2264 x < 13]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 5 \u2264 x < 7]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 5 < x \u2264 7]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 1 < x \u2264 13]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\nWhat is the solution to [MATH: \u2212 2 < (5x + 2)/(9) < 0?]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 20 < x < \u2212 2]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 4 < x < (7)/(5)]\nc. [MATH: 1 < x < (7)/(5)]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 4 < x < \u2212 (2)/(5)]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 (16)/(5) < x < (2)/(5)]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Compound Inequalities That Require Flipping the Inequality Sign\n\nExample Prompt:\nSolve the inequality [MATH: -1 < -2x + 1 \\leq 7.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe isolate [MATH: x] by applying the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n[MATH: \u2212 1|< \u2212 2x + 1 \u2264 7; \u2212 1 \u2212 1|< \u2212 2x + 1 \u2212 1 \u2264 7 \u2212 1; \u2212 2|< \u2212 2x \u2264 6]\n\nAt this point, we need to divide by [MATH: -2]. However, because we are dividing by a negative, we also need to flip the inequality signs.\n\n[MATH: \u2212 2|< \u2212 2x \u2264 6; (\u2212 2)/(\u2212 2)|>(\u2212 2x)/(\u2212 2)\u2265(6)/(\u2212 2); 1|> x \u2265 \u2212 3]\n\nThe solution can be written as [MATH: 1 > x \\geq - 3,] but this is somewhat difficult to read because the larger number is on the left rather than the right. So, we will rewrite the solution by reversing the order of the numbers:\n\n[MATH: -3 \\leq x < 1]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nSolve the inequality [MATH: \u2212 9 < \u2212 3x + 8 < \u2212 5.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (13)/(3) < x < (17)/(3)]\nb. [MATH: 13 < x < 17]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 13 < x < 17]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 (17)/(3) < x < (13)/(3)]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 (17)/(3) < x < \u2212 (13)/(3)]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\nWhat is the solution to [MATH: \u2212 10 < (6 \u2212 2x)/(3) < 0?]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 15 < x < 3]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 18 < x < 3]\nc. [MATH: 3 < x < 18]\nd. [MATH: 3 < x < 16]\ne. [MATH: 3 < x < 15]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Representing Solutions of Compound Inequalities on Number Lines\n\nExample Prompt:\nRepresent the solution to the inequality [MATH: -3 < 1-2x < 11] using a number line.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe isolate [MATH: x] by applying the addition and multiplication principles, as usual.\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle:\n\n[MATH: \u2212 3|< 1 \u2212 2x < 11; \u2212 3 \u2212 1|< 1 \u2212 2x \u2212 1 < 11 \u2212 1; \u2212 4|< \u2212 2x < 10]\n\nThen, we divide by [MATH: -2.] Note that since we are dividing by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality signs.\n\n[MATH: \u2212 4|< \u2212 2x < 10; (\u2212 4)/(\u2212 2)|> (\u2212 2x)/(\u2212 2) > (10)/(\u2212 2); 2|> x > \u2212 5]\n\nThe solution can be written as [MATH: 2 > x > -5], but this is somewhat difficult to read because the larger number is on the left rather than the right. So, we will rewrite the solution by reversing the order of the numbers:\n\n[MATH: -5 < x < 2]\n\nWe can represent this solution using a number line, as follows.\n\n[IMG: /graphics/692aff95e86f691ddb4f9957282f51c5]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\nWhich of the following number lines gives the solution to [MATH: \u2212 3 \u2264 2x \u2212 1 \u2264 5?]\n\nChoices:\na. [IMG: /graphics/q-44745-a-3]\nb. [IMG: /graphics/q-44745-a-5]\nc. [IMG: /graphics/q-44745-a-4]\nd. [IMG: /graphics/q-44745-a-1]\ne. [IMG: /graphics/q-44745-a-2]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\nWhich of the following number lines gives the solution to [MATH: \u2212 5 \u2264 \u2212 2x + 3 \u2264 5?]\n\nChoices:\na. [IMG: /graphics/q-44758-a-1]\nb. [IMG: /graphics/q-44758-a-5]\nc. [IMG: /graphics/q-44758-a-4]\nd. [IMG: /graphics/q-44758-a-2]\ne. [IMG: /graphics/q-44758-a-3]"
  }
